"""Repeat the S3 agent post-diagnostic behavior demo, aggregate, and report.

This runner drives ``scripts/demo-s3-agent-loop`` for a configurable number
of repetitions, archives every transcript, merges the harness'
``--results-json`` payloads into a single ``trials.json``, computes k/N rates
with Wilson 95% confidence intervals, and renders a fully self-contained,
accessible HTML report. The report can also be regenerated deterministically
from an existing ``trials.json`` with ``--from-data`` (no sessions are run).

The experiment measures how an agent behaves *after* a cloudformation-validate
diagnostic: whether it revises the request and reaches a clean validation or
success. Exactly three agent profiles are compared: a baseline told nothing
about validation; a validate-only factual profile told only the neutral fact
that the global ``--validate-only`` flag runs cloudformation-validate and
sends no request; and a validate-only guided profile that adds prescriptive
diagnostic-handling guidance. Whether the agent uses ``--validate-only`` is a
secondary mechanism, never the objective.

Safety is a synthetic, local boundary enforced by the harness: every child
AWS command runs with synthetic credentials only, IMDS and retries disabled,
and every permitted request pinned to a localhost fake AWS endpoint that
requires the exact synthetic signer and allows only s3api create-bucket and
cloudformation create-stack. The guarantee verified here is narrow: across
every observed agent AWS command, no real AWS credentials are used and no
remote AWS service is called; this is not a claim of OS-level zero egress.
The fail-closed cloudformation-validate hook is tooling, not agent behavior.
This runner neither talks to AWS nor bypasses any harness control, and it
restores the exact startup bytes of the agent profiles between repetitions.
"""

import argparse
import datetime
import hashlib
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import json
import math
import os
import re
import shutil
import statistics
import subprocess
import sys
